{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>And they beheld Elohim, and they ate and drank.</b> Rashi explained that they were gazing upon Him with a coarse heart, in the midst of eating and drinking. And his words are difficult, for he should have said first 'and they ate and drank' and afterward 'and they beheld Elohim.' And the Targum renders 'and they rejoiced in their offerings' — this too does not sit well on the tongue. And it would have been more correct to explain that there was no eating and drinking there at all, but rather they were a pattern of Moshe, who stood on the mountain forty days without eating and drinking and was sustained from the radiance of the Shekhinah. So too the nobles of the children of Israel — since they beheld Elohim, therefore it seemed as though they had eaten and drunk, for the sight of the face of the Shekhinah was their eating. However, from the fact that it says 'and upon the nobles of the children of Israel He did not stretch out His hand' (Exodus 24:11), one may press: why did He call them nobles (atzilim)? And furthermore, this verse needs to be connected with 'and they ate and drank.'" "And it appears to me that it comes to indicate the difference between the prophecy of Moshe and their prophecy. For to Moshe, HaShem sent the hand of His prophecy without intermediary — for prophecy is called 'the hand of HaShem,' as it is written (Ezekiel 37:1), 'the hand of HaShem was upon me' — and this matter is through ruach ha-kodesh. And because Moshe was near to the Shekhinah, therefore he was not permitted to attend to the needs of the body at all during the entire time he stood before HaShem, and this is what it says: 'and the cloud covered him six days' (Exodus 24:16). And in tractate Yoma (4a) our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said that this covering was in order to purge the food and drink within his intestines, so that he might stand alive before HaShem pure and cleansed of all matters of corporeality. And for this reason he did not eat for forty days, in order to distance him from all matters of the body that were created during the six days of Genesis: 'and He called to Moshe on the seventh day' (Exodus 24:16) — for the seventh is holy to HaShem, and the Sabbath day testifies to this. But those elders — Nadav and Avihu and the seventy elders — were of the atzilim upon whom was drawn from the ruach ha-kodesh that was upon Moshe and placed upon them; therefore they were not like Moshe in his prophecy, who did not eat or drink when he beheld the face of Elohim, but rather they beheld Elohim and they ate and drank (Exodus 24:11), for they made use of both parts — the part of the intellect and the part of matter: from the side of the intellect they beheld Elohim, and from the side of matter they ate and drank. And because they beheld a vision of Shaddai in the midst of eating and drinking, therefore they did not see in the illuminating speculum like Moshe — for the face of Moshe was like the face of the sun, which illuminates from every side; so too Moshe illuminated from both his parts, for even his matter became pure and clean like the clear sun, and it did not prevent him, through his body, from seeing a vision of Shaddai in the illuminating speculum. But the face of Yehoshua, who was of those upon whom the spirit was drawn, was like the moon, which is dark on one side and illuminated on the other; so too he was pure and clean from the side of the intellect, and in any case 'he did not depart from within the camp' (Exodus 33:11) — being like one of the people of the camp, from the side of matter, attending to the needs of the body; and he was like these atzilim of whom it is said: 'and they beheld Elohim and they ate and drank.'" "And according to the words of Rashi there is a measure of proof, from what is written (Leviticus 16:1–2) 'after the death of the two sons of Aharon' and 'he shall not come at all times into the sanctuary,' etc. Since HaShem, blessed be He, commanded him that he should not come into the sanctuary except on a day in which there is no eating and drinking — on Yom HaKippurim — hear from this that the sons of Aharon sinned in this very matter: they gazed upon the Shekhinah with a coarse heart of eating and drinking. And the Holy One, blessed be He, did not wish to mingle the joy of the Torah, and so He did not send a hand against them now, until the day of His visitation. And this matter will be explained further below in parashat Acharei Mot, with the help of HaShem, blessed be His name."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:22:45.800177869Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}